WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.678
Drone operations are the planned and controlled use of an unmanned aircraft

00:00:04.678 --> 00:00:06.967
system to accomplish a defined purpose.

00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:11.927
The system includes more than the aircraft: it also includes the controller, software,

00:00:11.927 --> 00:00:16.123
communications, batteries, sensors, payloads, records, and people responsible for the flight.

00:00:16.123 --> 00:00:21.465
Operator roles may include a pilot in command, visual observer, payload operator, mission lead,

00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:22.609
or data reviewer.

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:26.806
Even a small recreational flight concentrates several responsibilities in one person.

00:00:26.806 --> 00:00:31.765
Each role needs a shared understanding of the plan, limits, and emergency actions.

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:35.581
Drones support recreation, inspection, mapping, photography, research, public safety, security,

00:00:35.581 --> 00:00:37.488
and many other legitimate uses.

00:00:37.488 --> 00:00:43.211
The same aircraft may be suitable for one mission and inappropriate for another because weather,

00:00:43.211 --> 00:00:45.881
airspace, people, payload, or data sensitivity differ.

00:00:45.881 --> 00:00:47.789
Successful operation begins before takeoff.

00:00:47.789 --> 00:00:52.367
The operator defines the purpose, verifies the aircraft and crew are ready,

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:55.801
reviews the location and current official requirements, assesses hazards,

00:00:55.801 --> 00:00:58.471
and selects conservative limits for the mission.

00:00:58.471 --> 00:01:01.142
Safety, legality, and accountability are core principles.

00:01:01.142 --> 00:01:06.101
Technical capability does not create permission to fly anywhere or collect any data.

00:01:06.101 --> 00:01:09.917
Operators must respect aviation rules, property, privacy, people, other aircraft,

00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:12.969
and the limits of their equipment and training.

00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:17.165
The goal is a useful flight completed without creating unacceptable risk.

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:22.125
Good operators are willing to delay, change, or cancel a mission when conditions,

00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:25.940
authorization, equipment, or crew readiness do not support safe operation.
